FAILURE MODE, filed by scheduler 2026-07-28 (human-directed): a claim about machine state outlived its verification and was ACTED ON. Specifics, so this is reproducible rather than a moral: scheduler's .scheduler/QUESTIONS.md asserted on 2026-07-26 that three ~/.local/bin scripts were installed COPIES, with a real verified-at-the-time witness. On 2026-07-27 an /ideate pass read that entry, asked Zach to decide copies-vs-symlinks, got the answer 'symlink all three', and queued it as work. On 2026-07-28 'ls -l' showed all four had ALREADY been symlinks since 07-26/27 -- the work was queued against a state that no longer existed, and a human decision was spent on a non-question. Nothing caught it even though bin/deploy-drift-check.sh runs inside 'scheduler sweep' and knew the true state: detector output and prose claims never meet. WHAT SCHEDULER BUILT IN RESPONSE (its own half, done): bin/questions-lint.sh now FLAGs any QUESTIONS.md entry that asserts machine state (symlink/copies/installed/crontab/enabled=/reachable/expired/deploy key) without carrying a 'verified <date> via <command>' stamp -- it fired 6 times on scheduler's own file on first run. That makes CLAUDE.md's existing prose rule ('claims about system state re-probed, not quoted -- and if written down, stamped') executable for QUESTIONS.md only. WHAT IS YOURS: (1) the same rule belongs in hygiene-lint for FOCUS.md, BLOCKERS.md and DESIGN-NOTES.md across all 18 projects, which is where most state claims actually live; (2) the deeper regulator -- when a detector EXISTS for a claim's subject, prose restating that claim is redundant and should be replaced by a pointer to the detector, not re-verified by hand. Realisateur owns cross-project rationalization; scheduler can only fix its own file.
